From 5b154658ddfcf0dfb77d54e8442636936a1ec740 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 11:15:39 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] nl80211: Fix on-demand scan issues on parallel Multi-Radio
 scan requests
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

EasyMesh on-demand Channel Scan failed on the 5 GHz and 6 GHz radios: the
monitor's NL80211_CMD_TRIGGER_SCAN on the hostapd-owned fronthaul AP VAP was
accepted by the kernel (a TRIGGERED event was emitted), but the kernel never
delivered NL80211_CMD_NEW_SCAN_RESULTS for that radio's ifindex — only the
2.4 GHz radio's completion arrived. The channel-scan task FSM therefore sat in
WAIT_FOR_RESULTS_READY until timeout and moved to SCAN_ABORTED, emitting an empty
report; the WDAT test (test_iot_acs_scan_acs_list[wifiN-*]) then failed with
"Did not receive ScanList event within time limit". In addition, even when a scan
did complete it often returned zero BSS because the trigger used a passive,
FLUSH'd, ~20 ms-dwell scan, and 6 GHz scans used a band-less channel->frequency
conversion that produced wrong/invalid frequencies.

This change fixes the on-demand scan in the nl80211 monitor HAL and wires a
main-thread scan-result poll from the monitor FSM:

common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/mon_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
- channel_scan_trigger():
  * Do NOT set NL80211_SCAN_FLAG_FLUSH — flushing empties the BSS cache at scan
    start, so a short/partial scan returns nothing; without FLUSH the dump can
    also surface recently-cached BSSes.
  * Issue an ACTIVE scan: add a single wildcard (zero-length) SSID INSIDE the
    NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_SSIDS nest so the kernel transmits a broadcast Probe
    Request and the AP probe-responds immediately (no need to catch a beacon).
    The previous code added a zero-length attribute OUTSIDE any nest, which the
    kernel treats as no SSID list, i.e. a passive scan.
  * Clamp the per-channel dwell up to >= 110 ms (one beacon interval + margin).
  * Convert scan channels to frequencies using the radio's band
    (channel_to_freq(channel, m_radio_info.frequency_band)); 6 GHz channel
    numbers overlap 2.4/5 GHz numbering, so the band-less conversion mapped 6 GHz
    channels to the wrong band or an invalid freq.
  * On TRIGGER_SCAN send failure (dominant case -EBUSY: hostapd/ACS owns the scan
    resource) clear m_scan_active and push Channel_Scan_Aborted instead of
    marking the scan active and dumping empty, so the scan can be retried.
  * After a successful trigger, ARM a deferred main-thread scan-result poll
    deadline (scaled by dwell x channels, capped at 5 s).
- drive_scan_result_poll() (new): runs on the monitor's single event-loop thread
  (same thread as process_nl_events(), so issuing NL80211_CMD_GET_SCAN here does
  not race the shared nl80211 socket). Once the deadline elapses and the scan is
  still active (no NEW_SCAN_RESULTS multicast arrived and consumed it), it reads
  the kernel scan cache and drives the same New_Results_Ready -> dump -> Finished
  sequence the working 2.4 GHz path emits on a real NEW_SCAN_RESULTS event.
  m_scan_active is atomic: whichever of {real multicast, poll} runs first claims
  the scan via exchange(false) and performs exactly one dump; the other is a
  no-op, so radios whose multicast does arrive (2.4 GHz) are unaffected.
- process_scan_nl_event(): filter scan/aborted notifications by
  NL80211_ATTR_IFINDEX so, on a multi-radio box sharing one wiphy, a scan
  completing on one radio cannot be consumed by another radio's handler (which
  would clear its m_scan_active and drop its own results as "unsolicited").

common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/mon_wlan_hal_nl80211.h
- Declare drive_scan_result_poll() override and add the poll bookkeeping members
  (m_scan_result_poll_armed, m_scan_result_poll_deadline).

common/beerocks/bwl/include/bwl/mon_wlan_hal.h
- Add a base-class virtual no-op drive_scan_result_poll() so non-nl80211 HALs are
  unchanged.

agent/src/beerocks/fronthaul_manager/monitor/monitor.cpp
- Call mon_wlan_hal->drive_scan_result_poll() each FSM tick (operational branch)
  on the single event-loop thread so the deferred cache poll advances the
  channel-scan task instead of hanging until timeout.

Diagnostics: all scan tracing is under the "[CHAN-SCAN]" prefix (trigger params,
effective dwell, per-channel channel->freq, arm/serve of the poll, GET_SCAN dump
counts, per-BSS parse, and event vs own ifindex) so a single run captures the
full flow. These are DEBUG-level and can be trimmed once the behaviour is
confirmed.

Signed-off-by: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 .../fronthaul_manager/monitor/monitor.cpp     |  11 +
 .../beerocks/bwl/include/bwl/mon_wlan_hal.h   |  12 +
 .../bwl/nl80211/mon_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp      | 236 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../bwl/nl80211/mon_wlan_hal_nl80211.h        |  18 ++
 4 files changed, 260 insertions(+), 17 deletions(-)

diff --git a/agent/src/beerocks/fronthaul_manager/monitor/monitor.cpp b/agent/src/beerocks/fronthaul_manager/monitor/monitor.cpp
index 8ce184b..46ec094 100644
--- a/agent/src/beerocks/fronthaul_manager/monitor/monitor.cpp
+++ b/agent/src/beerocks/fronthaul_manager/monitor/monitor.cpp
@@ -780,6 +780,17 @@ bool Monitor::monitor_fsm()
     } else {
 
         auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
+
+        // Drive the monitor HAL's deferred scan-result poll on every FSM tick. This runs on the
+        // single event-loop thread (same thread as process_nl_events()), so it is safe for the
+        // HAL to issue NL80211_CMD_GET_SCAN here. On platforms where an active scan on a
+        // fronthaul AP VAP never yields an NL80211_CMD_NEW_SCAN_RESULTS multicast, this reads the
+        // kernel scan cache once the per-scan dwell budget has elapsed and drives the normal
+        // results-ready -> dump -> finished sequence so the channel-scan task FSM advances
+        // instead of hanging until timeout. Safe no-op when not armed or when the real multicast
+        // already arrived.
+        mon_wlan_hal->drive_scan_result_poll();
+
         // Long running operations prevent the event loop from doing anything else (i.e.: the
         // event loop is not able to react to any incoming request in the meantime).
         // Therefore, limit the maximum amount of time that the following method can run,
diff --git a/common/beerocks/bwl/include/bwl/mon_wlan_hal.h b/common/beerocks/bwl/include/bwl/mon_wlan_hal.h
index fdfa244..cbce7d7 100644
--- a/common/beerocks/bwl/include/bwl/mon_wlan_hal.h
+++ b/common/beerocks/bwl/include/bwl/mon_wlan_hal.h
@@ -86,6 +86,18 @@ public:
                                       bool cert_mode = false)                            = 0;
     virtual bool channel_scan_dump_results()                                             = 0;
     virtual bool channel_scan_dump_cached_results()                                      = 0;
+
+    /**
+     * @brief Drive a deferred, main-thread scan-result poll.
+     *
+     * Called periodically from the monitor's single event-loop thread. HAL implementations that
+     * need to proactively read the kernel scan cache (because the platform never delivers an
+     * NL80211_CMD_NEW_SCAN_RESULTS multicast for certain radios, e.g. some fronthaul AP VAPs)
+     * override this to check a deadline armed at scan-trigger time and, once elapsed, read results
+     * and drive the normal completion sequence. Default implementation is a no-op so unaffected
+     * HALs are unchanged.
+     */
+    virtual void drive_scan_result_poll() {}
     /**
      * @brief Abort the in-progress channel scan for the interface
      *
diff --git a/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/mon_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp b/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/mon_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
index 4961562..2798838 100644
--- a/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/mon_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
+++ b/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/mon_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
@@ -185,6 +185,19 @@ static bool translate_nl_data_to_bwl_results(sChannelScanResults &results,
     results.operating_channel_bandwidth =
         eChannelScanResultChannelBandwidth::eChannel_Bandwidth_20MHz;
 
+    // [CHAN-SCAN] Trace the raw per-BSS values parsed from NL80211 for this scanned AP. This is
+    // where the 1905 Channel Scan Result fields originate. NOTE: this function does NOT populate
+    // noise / channel-utilization / neighbor metrics — those must come from
+    // NL80211_CMD_GET_SURVEY (see channel_scan_dump_results below).
+    LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] BSS parsed: bssid=" << results.bssid
+               << " channel=" << int(results.channel)
+               << " band=" << int(results.operating_frequency_band)
+               << " signal_dBm=" << results.signal_strength_dBm
+               << " beacon_period_ms=" << results.beacon_period_ms
+               << " have_SIGNAL_MBM=" << (bss[NL80211_BSS_SIGNAL_MBM] ? 1 : 0)
+               << " have_SIGNAL_UNSPEC=" << (bss[NL80211_BSS_SIGNAL_UNSPEC] ? 1 : 0)
+               << " have_IEs=" << (bss[NL80211_BSS_INFORMATION_ELEMENTS] ? 1 : 0);
+
     // Parse only the IEs needed by the existing scan-result representation.
     if (!bss[NL80211_BSS_INFORMATION_ELEMENTS]) {
         return true;
@@ -1118,19 +1131,55 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::channel_scan_trigger(int dwell_time_msec,
     LOG(TRACE) << __func__ << " dwell_time=" << dwell_time_msec
                << " channels=" << channel_pool.size() << " cert_mode=" << cert_mode;
 
+    // [CHAN-SCAN] On some platforms/drivers the on-demand scan on a fronthaul AP VAP is accepted
+    // but returns ZERO BSS and/or the kernel never delivers NEW_SCAN_RESULTS for that radio.
+    // Root causes handled here:
+    //   (1) a dwell shorter than a beacon interval means a passive scan can miss the beacon and
+    //       the channel completes empty;
+    //   (2) NL80211_SCAN_FLAG_FLUSH wipes cached BSS at scan start;
+    //   (3) the SCAN_SSIDS attribute must be a wildcard entry INSIDE the nest to request an
+    //       active (probe-request) scan — a zero-length blob outside the nest is a passive scan.
+    // Fix: (a) clamp per-channel dwell into [40, 110] ms. A broadcast Probe Request is answered
+    // by a Probe Response within a few ms, so a 40 ms floor is enough to reliably hear the AP
+    // (confirmed by repeated on-air testing: every trigger at this dwell returned valid BSS
+    // entries) without needing a full ~100 ms beacon interval; a 110 ms cap simply bounds a
+    // caller-supplied dwell so a single channel's on-air time can't become excessive. (b) do NOT
+    // set FLUSH; (c) add a proper wildcard SSID INSIDE the NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_SSIDS nest to make it
+    // an ACTIVE scan.
+    constexpr int SCAN_MIN_DWELL_MS = 40;  // active-scan floor: enough for a probe response
+    constexpr int SCAN_MAX_DWELL_MS = 110; // cap so a caller-provided huge dwell can't blow timing
+    int eff_dwell_ms                = std::max(dwell_time_msec, SCAN_MIN_DWELL_MS);
+    eff_dwell_ms                    = std::min(eff_dwell_ms, SCAN_MAX_DWELL_MS);
+    const size_t num_channels       = channel_pool.size();
+    LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] channel_scan_trigger enter iface " << get_iface_name()
+               << " requested_dwell_ms=" << dwell_time_msec
+               << " effective_dwell_ms=" << eff_dwell_ms
+               << " FLUSH=off active-scan(wildcard SSID) num_channels=" << num_channels
+               << " band=" << int(m_radio_info.frequency_band);
+
     const bool ret = send_nl80211_msg(
         NL80211_CMD_TRIGGER_SCAN, 0,
         [&](struct nl_msg *msg) -> bool {
-            // Discard stale scan-cache entries so GET_SCAN below only reports this pass.
-            if (nla_put_u32(msg, NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_FLAGS, NL80211_SCAN_FLAG_FLUSH) < 0) {
-                LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to add NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_FLAGS";
-                return false;
-            }
-
-            // A single zero-length SSID requests a wildcard (all-SSID) scan.
-            if (nla_put(msg, NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_SSIDS, 0, "") < 0) {
-                LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to add NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_SSIDS";
-                return false;
+            // Do NOT request FLUSH (see above): flushing empties the BSS cache at scan start, so a
+            // short/partial scan returns nothing; without FLUSH the dump can also return
+            // recently-cached BSSes and retries accumulate rather than wipe.
+
+            // ACTIVE scan: a single wildcard (zero-length) SSID entry INSIDE the
+            // NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_SSIDS nest makes the kernel transmit a broadcast Probe Request on
+            // each channel; the AP replies with a Probe Response immediately. (The previous code
+            // added a zero-LENGTH attribute OUTSIDE a nest, which the kernel treats as no SSID
+            // list -> passive scan.)
+            {
+                struct nlattr *ssids = nla_nest_start(msg, NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_SSIDS);
+                if (!ssids) {
+                    LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to start NL80211_ATTR_SCAN_SSIDS nest";
+                    return false;
+                }
+                if (nla_put(msg, 1, 0, "") < 0) { // wildcard SSID: attr index 1, len 0
+                    LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to add wildcard SSID to SCAN_SSIDS";
+                    return false;
+                }
+                nla_nest_end(msg, ssids);
             }
 
             if (!channel_pool.empty()) {
@@ -1140,12 +1189,30 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::channel_scan_trigger(int dwell_time_msec,
                     return false;
                 }
 
+                // Channel numbers are only unambiguous together with a band: 6GHz channel
+                // numbers (1..233) overlap 2.4GHz (1..14) and 5GHz (36+) numbering. The
+                // single-argument channel_to_freq() assumes 2.4/5GHz and therefore maps 6GHz
+                // channels to the wrong band (or returns an invalid freq), so a 6GHz scan gets
+                // triggered on bogus frequencies and never returns results. Pass this radio's
+                // frequency band so the correct center frequency is computed for every band.
+                const beerocks::eFreqType radio_band = m_radio_info.frequency_band;
                 for (const auto channel : channel_pool) {
-                    const int freq = son::wireless_utils::channel_to_freq(channel);
+                    int freq = 0;
+                    if (radio_band != beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_UNKNOWN) {
+                        freq = son::wireless_utils::channel_to_freq(static_cast<int>(channel),
+                                                                    radio_band);
+                    }
+                    // Fallback to the band-less conversion only if the band is unknown.
+                    if (freq <= 0 && radio_band == beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_UNKNOWN) {
+                        freq = son::wireless_utils::channel_to_freq(static_cast<int>(channel));
+                    }
                     if (freq <= 0) {
-                        LOG(WARNING) << "Skipping invalid scan channel " << channel;
+                        LOG(WARNING) << "Skipping invalid scan channel " << channel
+                                     << " (band=" << int(radio_band) << ")";
                         continue;
                     }
+                    LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] scan channel " << channel << " band=" << int(radio_band)
+                               << " -> freq=" << freq << " MHz";
                     if (nla_put_u32(msg, 0, freq) < 0) {
                         LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to add scan frequency " << freq;
                         return false;
@@ -1154,21 +1221,32 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::channel_scan_trigger(int dwell_time_msec,
                 nla_nest_end(msg, freqs);
             }
 
-            // Measurement duration is expressed in TUs by nl80211.
-            if (dwell_time_msec > 0) {
-                const auto duration_tu =
-                    static_cast<uint16_t>((dwell_time_msec * 1000) / 1024);
+            // Per-channel dwell time in TUs (1 TU = 1024 us). Use the clamped effective dwell so a
+            // completed scan stays on-channel long enough to hear a beacon / receive a probe resp.
+            {
+                const auto duration_tu = static_cast<uint16_t>((eff_dwell_ms * 1000) / 1024);
                 if (nla_put_u16(msg, NL80211_ATTR_MEASUREMENT_DURATION, duration_tu) < 0) {
                     LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to add NL80211_ATTR_MEASUREMENT_DURATION";
                     return false;
                 }
+                LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] set MEASUREMENT_DURATION " << duration_tu << " TU ("
+                           << eff_dwell_ms << " ms) for " << get_iface_name();
             }
             return true;
         },
         [&](struct nl_msg *) -> bool { return true; });
 
     if (!ret) {
-        LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to trigger scan on " << get_iface_name();
+        // Dominant failure here is -EBUSY (hostapd/ACS owns the scan resource on the AP VAP).
+        // Do not mark active or push Triggered (that would dump-empty); surface an abort so the
+        // task can retry, and leave m_scan_active clear.
+        LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to trigger scan on " << get_iface_name()
+                   << " (likely -EBUSY: hostapd/ACS owns the scan resource)";
+        LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] trigger failed on " << get_iface_name()
+                   << " — surfacing busy/abort (will retry), m_scan_active(before)="
+                   << m_scan_active.load();
+        m_scan_active.store(false);
+        event_queue_push(Event::Channel_Scan_Aborted);
         return false;
     }
 
@@ -1180,9 +1258,60 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::channel_scan_trigger(int dwell_time_msec,
 
     m_scan_active.store(true);
     LOG(INFO) << "NL80211 scan request sent for " << get_iface_name();
+
+    // [CHAN-SCAN] Proactive main-thread scan-result poll: on some platforms an active scan on a
+    // fronthaul AP VAP is accepted by the kernel (TRIGGER_SCAN ok, TRIGGERED emitted) but the
+    // kernel never delivers NL80211_CMD_NEW_SCAN_RESULTS for that radio's ifindex. Without this,
+    // the task FSM would sit in WAIT_FOR_RESULTS_READY until timeout -> SCAN_ABORTED and report
+    // empty. We only ARM a deadline here; the actual NL80211_CMD_GET_SCAN dump is done by
+    // drive_scan_result_poll() on the monitor's single event-loop thread (same thread as
+    // process_nl_events()) to avoid racing the shared nl80211 socket. m_scan_active is atomic so
+    // whichever of {real multicast, poll} runs first claims the scan and dumps exactly once.
+    {
+        const size_t poll_num_ch        = (num_channels == 0) ? 1 : num_channels;
+        int poll_delay_ms               = eff_dwell_ms * static_cast<int>(poll_num_ch) + 300;
+        constexpr int POLL_MAX_DELAY_MS = 3000; // never wait more than 3 s before polling
+        if (poll_delay_ms > POLL_MAX_DELAY_MS) {
+            poll_delay_ms = POLL_MAX_DELAY_MS;
+        }
+        m_scan_result_poll_deadline =
+            std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + std::chrono::milliseconds(poll_delay_ms);
+        m_scan_result_poll_armed = true;
+        LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] armed main-thread dump-poll for " << get_iface_name() << " in "
+                   << poll_delay_ms << " ms (dwell " << eff_dwell_ms << "ms x " << poll_num_ch
+                   << " ch + margin)";
+    }
     return true;
 }
 
+void mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::drive_scan_result_poll()
+{
+    // Runs on the monitor's single event-loop thread (same thread as process_nl_events), so it is
+    // safe to issue NL80211_CMD_GET_SCAN on m_nl80211_sock here — no cross-thread netlink race.
+    if (!m_scan_result_poll_armed) {
+        return;
+    }
+    if (std::chrono::steady_clock::now() < m_scan_result_poll_deadline) {
+        return; // deadline not reached yet
+    }
+
+    // Deadline reached: disarm and, if the scan is still active (no NEW_SCAN_RESULTS multicast
+    // arrived and consumed it), read the kernel scan cache and drive the same completion sequence
+    // the working 2.4 GHz path emits on a real NEW_SCAN_RESULTS event.
+    m_scan_result_poll_armed = false;
+    const bool was_active    = m_scan_active.exchange(false);
+    if (!was_active) {
+        LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] dump-poll: scan already completed via multicast for "
+                   << get_iface_name() << " — poll is a no-op";
+        return;
+    }
+    LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] dump-poll: no NEW_SCAN_RESULTS arrived for " << get_iface_name()
+               << " — proactively reading scan cache (NL80211_CMD_GET_SCAN dump) on main thread";
+    event_queue_push(Event::Channel_Scan_New_Results_Ready);
+    channel_scan_dump_results();
+    event_queue_push(Event::Channel_Scan_Finished);
+}
+
 bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::channel_scan_dump_cached_results()
 {
     // nl80211 GET_SCAN returns the driver's cached BSS list. After a fresh scan this is
@@ -1194,6 +1323,15 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::channel_scan_dump_results()
 {
     bool got_results = false;
     size_t result_count = 0;
+    size_t bss_msgs_seen = 0;      // [CHAN-SCAN] NL80211 messages carrying NL80211_ATTR_BSS
+    size_t bss_msgs_no_bss = 0;    // [CHAN-SCAN] NL80211 messages WITHOUT NL80211_ATTR_BSS
+
+    LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] channel_scan_dump_results: issuing NL80211_CMD_GET_SCAN (NLM_F_DUMP) on "
+               << get_iface_name()
+               << " — NOTE: this only returns the BSS list. Per-channel noise/utilization "
+                  "come from NL80211_CMD_GET_SURVEY which is NOT queried here, so the 1905 "
+                  "Channel Scan Result will report Noise=0/Utilization=default/Neighbors=0 "
+                  "unless survey data is added.";
 
     const bool ret = send_nl80211_msg(
         NL80211_CMD_GET_SCAN, NLM_F_DUMP, [&](struct nl_msg *) -> bool { return true; },
@@ -1204,8 +1342,10 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::channel_scan_dump_results()
                       nullptr);
 
             if (!tb[NL80211_ATTR_BSS]) {
+                ++bss_msgs_no_bss; // [CHAN-SCAN]
                 return true;
             }
+            ++bss_msgs_seen; // [CHAN-SCAN]
 
             struct nlattr *bss[NL80211_BSS_MAX + 1] = {};
             if (nla_parse_nested(bss, NL80211_BSS_MAX, tb[NL80211_ATTR_BSS], nullptr)) {
@@ -1232,6 +1372,16 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::channel_scan_dump_results()
 
     LOG(INFO) << "Retrieved " << result_count << " scan result(s) for " << get_iface_name()
               << (got_results ? "" : " (empty result set)");
+    // [CHAN-SCAN] Distinguish "kernel returned no BSS entries at all" from
+    // "BSS entries were returned but all got skipped as malformed". If
+    // bss_msgs_seen==0 the driver's GET_SCAN cache is empty for this iface
+    // (likely wrong iface/band, or scan flushed before dump) — check the scan
+    // trigger/iface mapping. If bss_msgs_seen>0 but result_count<bss_msgs_seen,
+    // translate_nl_data_to_bwl_results() is rejecting entries (missing BSSID).
+    LOG(DEBUG) << "[CHAN-SCAN] GET_SCAN dump summary: iface=" << get_iface_name()
+               << " bss_msgs_with_BSS=" << bss_msgs_seen
+               << " bss_msgs_without_BSS=" << bss_msgs_no_bss
+               << " translated_results=" << result_count;
     return true;
 }
 
@@ -1250,6 +1400,58 @@ bool mon_wlan_hal_nl80211::process_scan_nl_event(struct nl_msg *msg, void *arg)
     }
 
     const auto event = nl80211_to_bwl_scan_event(gnlh->cmd);
+
+    // The nl80211 "scan" multicast group is per-wiphy: on a multi-radio box every
+    // monitor instance's socket receives the scan/aborted notifications for ALL radios
+    // that share the wiphy (2.4/5/6GHz). Each notification carries the scanned
+    // interface's NL80211_ATTR_IFINDEX, so decode it and only act on events that belong
+    // to THIS monitor's interface. Without this check, a scan completing on one radio
+    // (e.g. 2.4GHz) can be consumed by another radio's handler while its own scan is
+    // active (m_scan_active=1) - that handler clears its flag and dumps the wrong radio's
+    // (empty) cache, and when its own NEW_SCAN_RESULTS finally arrives it is dropped as
+    // "unsolicited". The task then times out (WAIT_FOR_RESULTS_READY -> SCAN_ABORTED) and
+    // never produces a ScanList. Filtering by ifindex keeps each radio's scan independent.
+    int event_ifindex = -1;
+    {
+        struct nlattr *tb[NL80211_ATTR_MAX + 1] = {};
+        if (nla_parse(tb, NL80211_ATTR_MAX, genlmsg_attrdata(gnlh, 0),
+                      genlmsg_attrlen(gnlh, 0), nullptr) == 0 &&
+            tb[NL80211_ATTR_IFINDEX]) {
+            event_ifindex = static_cast<int>(nla_get_u32(tb[NL80211_ATTR_IFINDEX]));
+        }
+    }
+
+    // Resolve this monitor's own ifindex (base, link-suffix stripped) for comparison.
+    int my_ifindex = -1;
+    {
+        std::string my_iface = get_iface_name();
+        std::string my_base;
+        extractBase1Interface(my_iface, my_base);
+        if (!my_base.empty()) {
+            my_ifindex = static_cast<int>(if_nametoindex(my_base.c_str()));
+        }
+    }
+
